  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nEarly morning satellite imagery reveals increasing cloud cover \nand moisture streaming across the northern Great Basin as \neastern Idaho resides between a broad ridge over West Texas and \na digging upper-level trough over the Pacific Northwest. Strong \nsouthwesterly flow aloft will overspread the region with Pacific\nmoisture over the next few days, maintaining cool temperatures \nand high precipitation chances through the weekend. High- \nresolution convective-allowing models (CAMs) show widespread \nprecipitation filling in around sunrise, followed by a brief \nearly afternoon lull before a second, deeper moisture surge \narrives this evening and overnight. While overall convective \nparameters today are modest, stronger cells will remain capable \nof producing localized gusty winds and small hail. Increased \ncloud shading and falling 500 mb heights will cap today's \nmaximum temperatures in the 70s, with only isolated spots \nreaching the lower 80s.\n\nThe upper-level low will shift into central and eastern \nWashington on Saturday, continuing to lower H5 heights and \nholding daytime highs in the 60s and 70s. Despite the cool \nsurface readings, strong upper-level forcing will support \norganized convection. The Storm Prediction Center (SPC) has \ndesignated a Marginal Risk for severe storms across all of \neastern Idaho, with a Slight Risk focused across the far \nsoutheastern corner from Pocatello south toward the Utah border.\nThis region aligns with a focused corridor of strong QG ascent \nimmediately ahead of the main trough axis.\n\nShort-range guidance exhibits subtle thermodynamic variance: \nthe RAP depicts rich low-level moisture with dewpoints nearing \n60, mid-level lapse rates exceeding 7.5 C/km, and MLCAPE over \n2000 J/kg, while the NAM maintains drier surface dewpoints in \nthe upper 40s to 50s and slightly less steep lapse rates. \nNevertheless, both models agree on peak MLCAPE exceeding 1000 \nJ/kg alongside elongated hodographs and effective bulk shear \nover 40 knots, establishing an environment conducive to rotating\nsupercells capable of severe downburst winds.\n\nThe system begins to exit on Sunday as the primary low swings \nacross northern Montana and southern Alberta. Cloud cover and \nprecipitation coverage will diminish from west to east \nthroughout the day, while temperatures remain seasonably cool in\nthe 60s and 70s. Storm-total precipitation from Friday through \nSunday is forecast to range from around 0.10\" across the central\nmountains and western zones, 0.25\" to 0.50\" across the Snake \nPlain, and 0.50\" to 1.00\" across the Eastern Highlands. Given \nthe convective nature of the event, localized totals may vary \nbased on thunderstorm tracks.\n\nTemperatures will moderate slightly on Monday as weak ridging\nattempts to build overhead, allowing a few lower elevation spots\nto touch 80\u00b0F while most remain in the upper 70s. However,\nridging will remain short-lived as global guidance maintains\nbroad troughing over the Pacific Northwest throughout next week.\nMultiple shortwave impulses tracking through this cyclonic flow\nwill preserve low-end daily precipitation chances and hold\ntemperatures on the cool side of average. Overnight lows will\ndrop noticeably into the 40s and lower 50s across valley floors,\nushering in a crisp, fall-like environment for the start of\nSeptember.",
